meta { title = Installation, order = 2, group = Project }

Install the Kalandra CLI from npm, create a project, and run your first parallel program. Kalandra 0.13.0 uses Bun to run its compiler, local workers, and remote services.


h2 { Install Bun }

Use Bun 1.3.12 or newer. Bun is the runtime; npm is the package registry distributing Kalandra. Installing the CLI does not install Bun for you.

link { url = https://bun.sh/docs/installation, Install Bun for your operating system }

Confirm it is available in your terminal:

codeblock { language = bash, "bun --version" }

h2 { Install Kalandra }

Install the version used by this guide from the npm registry:

codeblock { language = bash, "bun add --global @kalandra/cli@0.13.0
kalandra --version
kalandra --help" }

The version output should identify Kalandra 0.13.0. If your shell cannot find kalandra, run `bun pm bin -g` and add the printed directory to your PATH, then open a new terminal.

h2 { Create and run a project }

Choose a directory where you keep your projects. The destination my-app must not already exist:

codeblock { language = bash, "kalandra new my-app
cd my-app
kalandra run src/main.kalandra --strict" }

The starter contains two modules and a typed parallel function. The result is [2, 4, 6]. Project creation is offline: it writes the starter without installing dependencies or initializing Git. You do not need to clone the compiler repository.

h2 { Check and build }

Check one source file without executing it:

codeblock { language = bash, "kalandra check src/math.kalandra --strict" }

Build the project and its imported modules as JavaScript:

codeblock { language = bash, "kalandra build src/main.kalandra -o build --strict" }

To rebuild when source files change:

codeblock { language = bash, "kalandra build src/main.kalandra -o build --strict --watch" }

Use `kalandra run` for managed parallel execution. It builds the program, invokes its exported main function, prints the result, and closes the workers. A project build writes JavaScript and source maps; it does not run the program or produce a portable deployment bundle.

h2 { Write your first program }

Source files use the .kalandra extension. Follow the first lesson to write and run a small program, then explore functions, records, and parallel calls.

link { url = /docs/guide/first-program, Write your first program }
link { url = /docs/playground, Try examples without installing }

h2 { Editor support }

Syntax highlighting and the language server are separate from the CLI. Editor integration is currently manual; a one-click editor extension is not part of this preview. The contributor guide describes the existing source and built language-server setup.

link { url = /docs/development#editor-support, Configure the language server }

h2 { Working on Kalandra itself }

You only need a toolchain checkout to contribute to the compiler, runtime, or website, or to run the repository's integration examples.

link { url = /docs/development, Contributing and source setup }
